  ],
  "hints": [
    "plain XOR cancels pairs, but here numbers appear three times, not two - does XOR alone still cancel everything except the answer? (no)",
    "think per bit position instead of per number - if you sum up bit i across every number in the array, what's that sum mod 3 for a bit that belongs to a triple, versus a bit that belongs to the single number?",
    "for any bit position, all the tripled numbers contribute a multiple of 3 to that position's total count (0 or 3), so the total count mod 3 exactly reveals whether the single number has that bit set",
    "for each of the 32 bit positions, count how many numbers have that bit set, take that count mod 3, and if it's 1, set that bit in your answer - or use the classic ones/twos two-variable trick to do it in one pass without 32 explicit loops"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming a plain XOR of the whole array still works when elements repeat three times instead of two - it doesn't",
    "using a hash map when the interviewer is testing whether you know the O(1) space bit-counting trick",
    "getting the mod-3 logic backwards and setting a bit when the count is divisible by 3 instead of when it isn't"
  ],
  "content": {
